# =====================================================================================
#  01 - NOT WORKING: native API Management MCP pass-through ("regular setup")
#  /ado-mcp-native/{{org}}
#
#  Walk the requests top to bottom: they are the steps a real MCP client (VS Code,
#  Claude Code with a client ID) performs. The flow ends at step 5 with Entra error
#  AADSTS9010010: this is the reported sign-in failure. Steps 7-10 show that the MCP
#  traffic itself works when a token is obtained some other way.
#
#  Variables come from the environment file (see http/README.md):
#    gateway  = https://<gateway-host>    org = <organization>
#    tenant   = <tenant-id>               clientId = <public-client-id>
#    token    = an Azure DevOps MCP token (only for steps 7-10)
#  Compare each step with 02-working-proxy.http: the step numbers line up.
# =====================================================================================

# Fixed, lab-only PKCE pair (RFC 7636). Never reuse a fixed verifier in a real client.
@codeVerifier = lab-only-pkce-verifier-0123456789-abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz
@codeChallenge = zaFp8oSoZN9fqMAEJGn4XTBBL02a9LeZix3gER7YRB4
@redirectUri = http://localhost
@mcpUrl = {{gateway}}/ado-mcp-native/{{org}}

###
# STEP 1 - Anonymous initialize
# WHAT  : The first MCP message (JSON-RPC "initialize"), sent without a token.
# WHY   : A client learns that authorization is needed only when the server answers 401
#         (MCP authorization 2025-06-18, "Authorization Server Discovery").
# EXPECT: HTTP 401, no body, and
#           WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata="https://<gateway-host>/ado-mcp-native/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/<org>"
#         The origin's challenge pointed at mcp.dev.azure.com. APIM's native pass-through
#         REWROTE it to the gateway (built in, cannot be changed by policy).
# LOGS  : x-apim-request-id response header; docs/kql/www-authenticate-rewrite.kql shows
#         the origin challenge next to the rewritten one.
# @name init
# @no-redirect
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"http-walkthrough","version":"1"}}}

###
# STEP 2 - Protected-resource metadata (PRM)
# WHAT  : Reads the document the resource_metadata URL from step 1 points to.
# WHY   : RFC 9728: the PRM names the protected resource ("resource"), its scopes and its
#         authorization server(s). Clients check that "resource" equals the URL they used.
# EXPECT: HTTP 200
#           { "resource": "https://<gateway-host>/ado-mcp-native/<org>",            <- rewritten by APIM
#             "authorization_servers": [ "https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0" ],
#             "bearer_methods_supported": [ "header" ],
#             "scopes_supported": [ "https://mcp.dev.azure.com/.default" ] }       <- NOT rewritten
#         The resource is the gateway, but the scope belongs to Azure DevOps: this
#         combination is what Entra rejects in step 5.
# LOGS  : policy-traces.kql, step native-inbound for this path.
# @name prm
GET {{gateway}}/ado-mcp-native/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/{{org}}
Accept: application/json

###
# STEP 3 - Authorization-server metadata
# WHAT  : Reads Entra's OpenID configuration, the authorization server named in the PRM.
# WHY   : RFC 8414 / OpenID Connect Discovery: the client needs the authorize and token
#         endpoints. The URL is built from step 2 (response chaining).
# EXPECT: HTTP 200 with "authorization_endpoint": ".../<tenant-id>/oauth2/v2.0/authorize",
#         "token_endpoint", and NO "registration_endpoint" (Entra has no dynamic client
#         registration). Clients that need DCR (Claude Code, MCP Inspector) stop here with
#         "Incompatible auth server: does not support dynamic client registration".
# @name asMetadata
GET {{prm.response.body.$.authorization_servers[0]}}/.well-known/openid-configuration
Accept: application/json

###
# STEP 4 - Client registration
# There is nothing to call: Entra has no registration endpoint. A client continues only
# if it was configured with a client ID ({{clientId}}, the public client created by
# scripts/setup-entra.ps1). In 02-working-proxy.http this step is POST /register.

###
# STEP 5 - Authorization request: THE FAILURE
# WHAT  : The URL a client opens in the browser to sign the user in, built as the MCP
#         spec requires: scope from the PRM, and resource = the PRM "resource" (RFC 8707).
# WHY   : RFC 8707 resource indicators bind the token to the resource from step 2.
# EXPECT: HTTP 302 straight away, before any sign-in page. Location:
#           http://localhost/?error=invalid_target&error_description=AADSTS9010010: The resource
#           parameter provided in the request doesn't match with the requested scopes. ...
#         Entra refuses a gateway URL as resource for an Azure DevOps scope.
#         Try it: change resource to https://mcp.dev.azure.com and Entra answers 200 with
#         its sign-in page instead. That is the translation the working setup does.
# LOGS  : Entra sign-in logs (no gateway call happens in this step).
# @name authorize
# @no-redirect
GET https://login.microsoftonline.com/{{tenant}}/oauth2/v2.0/authorize
    ?client_id={{clientId}}
    &response_type=code
    &redirect_uri={{redirectUri}}
    &scope=https%3A%2F%2Fmcp.dev.azure.com%2F.default
    &resource={{mcpUrl}}
    &code_challenge={{codeChallenge}}
    &code_challenge_method=S256
    &state=walkthrough-native

###
# STEP 6 - Token: not possible on this setup
# Sign-in stopped in step 5, so there is no code to exchange. To prove the rest of the
# MCP traffic works, steps 7-10 use a token obtained outside the MCP flow:
#   az account get-access-token --scope https://mcp.dev.azure.com/.default --query accessToken -o tsv
# Put it in the "token" variable of your private environment file (never commit it).

###
# STEP 7 - Authenticated initialize
# WHAT  : The same initialize as step 1, now with the user's Azure DevOps MCP token.
# WHY   : MCP lifecycle: initialize negotiates the protocol version and capabilities.
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, Content-Type: text/event-stream. REST Client shows the raw SSE text:
#           event: message
#           data: {"result":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18",...,"serverInfo":{"name":"AzureDevOps.Mcp",...}},"id":1,"jsonrpc":"2.0"}
#         The Azure DevOps server issues no Mcp-Session-Id header (it is stateless), so
#         later requests need no session header.
# LOGS  : mcp-methods.kql (ApiManagementGatewayMCPLog) shows the JSON-RPC method.
# @name initAuth
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"http-walkthrough","version":"1"}}}

###
# STEP 8 - notifications/initialized
# WHAT  : Tells the server the client finished initialization. A notification has no id.
# WHY   : MCP lifecycle, "Initialization".
# EXPECT: HTTP 202 Accepted, empty body.
# @name initialized
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"notifications/initialized"}

###
# STEP 9 - tools/list
# WHAT  : Discovers the tools the server offers.
# WHY   : MCP "Tools": clients call tools/list before tools/call.
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, SSE. data: {"result":{"tools":[{"name":"core_list_projects",...},...]}...}
#         26 read-only tools, because the gateway sends X-MCP-Readonly: true.
# @name toolsList
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/list"}

###
# STEP 10 - tools/call (read-only)
# WHAT  : Runs core_list_projects as the signed-in user.
# EXPECT: HTTP 200, SSE. data: {"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"[{\"id\":\"<project-id>\",\"name\":\"<project>\",...}]"}]},"id":3,...}
#         The data plane works and Azure DevOps sees the real user. Only sign-in is broken.
# STEP 11 (validation) - top: 500 is NOT rejected by the gateway (MCP servers cannot run
#         validate-content; neither setup validates). Change "top" to 500 and the server answers.
# LOGS  : mcp-calls-app-insights.kql (gen_ai.tool.name per call).
# @name toolsCall
POST {{mcpUrl}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"core_list_projects","arguments":{"top":5}}}
